    {
      "title": "numpy.std \u2014 NumPy v2.1 Manual",
      "url": "https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.std.html",
      "snippet": "Compute the standard deviation along the specified axis. NumPy notes that by default `ddof=0`, so the divisor is `N`, the number of elements; this corresponds to the population standard deviation formula \\(\\sqrt{\\sum (x_i-\\bar{x})^2 / N}\\). The docs also show `ddof=1` for the sample standard deviation.",
      "domain": "numpy.org",
      "rank": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Standard deviation in NumPy: ddof explained",
      "url": "https://realpython.com/numpy-standard-deviation/",
      "snippet": "When `ddof=0`, NumPy divides by `N` instead of `N-1`, which matches the population standard deviation formula. For example, for the array `[1, 2, 3, 4]`, the population standard deviation is 1.1180, while `ddof=1` gives the sample standard deviation 1.2910.",
      "domain": "realpython.com",
      "rank": 2
    },
    {
      "title": "Understanding the `ddof` parameter in NumPy statistics",
      "url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python-numpy-std-with-ddof-parameter/",
      "snippet": "The `ddof` argument stands for Delta Degrees of Freedom and changes the divisor used in the variance calculation. With `ddof=0`, the formula uses `N`; with `ddof=1`, it uses `N-1`, which is commonly used for sample estimates. A worked example with values 10, 12, 23, 23, 16, 23, 21 gives different results depending on the setting.",
      "domain": "geeksforgeeks.org",
      "rank": 3
    },
    {
      "title": "numpy.std() - Compute standard deviation of array elements",
      "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/python/numpy/numpy_std.asp",
      "snippet": "The NumPy `std()` function returns the standard deviation of the array elements, and the default `ddof=0` means the calculation uses the population denominator. In simple terms, the variance is divided by the total number of elements, `N`, before taking the square root.",
      "domain": "w3schools.com",
      "rank": 4
    },
    {
      "title": "Python NumPy Standard Deviation: Population vs Sample",
      "url": "https://www.statology.org/numpy-standard-deviation/",
      "snippet": "NumPy uses `ddof=0` by default, which produces the population standard deviation rather than the sample version. For the dataset `[4, 8, 6, 5, 3, 7, 2, 9]`, the population standard deviation is 2.2913 and the sample standard deviation is 2.4495.",
      "domain": "statology.org",
      "rank": 5
    }
  ],
  "query": "NumPy ddof=0 population standard deviation formula",
  "count": 5
}

